<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry Thuku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harry Thuku was a Kenyan political leader, one of the first to organise African protest against colonial rule. In 1921 he founded the Young Kikuyu Association and campaigned against forced labour, the pass system and land loss. His arrest in 1922 led to a protest in Nairobi in which police killed many people, and he was exiled until 1930.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-thuku.jpg" alt="Harry Thuku" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Editor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harry-Thuku-banner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thuku was born in Kiambu district of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> into a Kikuyu family; the Kikuyu were among the peoples who lost most land to white settlers. After four years at a mission school he became a typesetter for the Leader, a settler newspaper, and in 1918 a clerk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> operator in the government treasury office in Nairobi. He was one of the first Africans in Kenya fully able to work in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With Abdalla Tairara he founded the Young Kikuyu Association, a non-militant body concerned above all with keeping African-owned land. He served as secretary of the Kikuyu Association in 1920–1921 but left it in 1921, and in July of that year founded the East African Association in Nairobi, the first multi-ethnic political organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, which also drew in a number of women. The historian Robert I. Rotberg calls him the first Kikuyu nationalist.</p><p>He was arrested on 14 March 1922. Mary Mūthoni Nyanjiru led demonstrations on the following two days; on 16 March a crowd of 7,000 to 8,000 gathered at the Nairobi police station, and police, joined by white civilians, opened fire, killing at least 25 and possibly 56. Thuku was exiled without charge or trial to Kismayu. After his return he became president of the Kikuyu Central Association in 1932, regrouped loyal members of the East African Association as the Kikuyu Provincial Association in 1935, and in 1944 founded the Kenya African Study Union, forerunner of the Kenya African Union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His moderation divided him from the rising generation of Kenyan leaders. He opposed the Mau Mau movement, condemning it in a broadcast on 12 December 1952, and on 28 January 1954 joined twenty-two other Kikuyu leaders at Kabete in an appeal to renounce it. He then farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> at Kabete, was one of the first Kikuyu granted a coffee licence, and in 1959 became the first African board member of the Kenya Planters Coffee Union. He died on 14 June 1970, and Harry Thuku Road in Nairobi bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harry Thuku” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>African political protest began with <b>Harry Thuku</b> in 1921 and grew through Kikuyu associations.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya-colony">Kenya Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Nairobi, in what is now <a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-1.29,36.82&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Nairobi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Harry Thuku"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5581 5110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5581 5110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-1.29,36.82&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
